---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import SourcesStandards from '../../components/SourcesStandards.astro';
import { articleSchema, gitDates } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';

// This hub carried no schema or named author at all before the 2026-10-22
// audit — one of the 8 core YMYL pages the audit named specifically.
const safetySourcePath = 'src/pages/safety/index.astro';
const jsonLd = articleSchema({
  sourcePath: safetySourcePath,
  path: '/safety',
  headline: 'Oil Rig Safety — Hazards, PPE, and Emergency Systems',
  description: "Complete oil rig safety reference: major hazard categories, required PPE, H2S safety, and emergency response systems.",
  lang: 'en',
});
const { dateModified: safetyDateModified } = gitDates(safetySourcePath);
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Oil Rig Safety — Hazards, PPE, and Emergency Systems"
  description="Complete oil rig safety reference: major hazard categories, required PPE, H2S safety, and emergency response systems."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Safety', href: '/safety' }]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/safety"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/safety" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">10 — HSE</div>
    <h1>Oil Rig <span>Safety</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">The hazards, the equipment, and the systems that keep people alive on one of the most demanding industrial worksites in the world. If you're starting out as a <a href="/careers/roustabout" style="color:var(--amber);">roustabout</a> or <a href="/careers/roughneck" style="color:var(--amber);">roughneck</a>, this page — not the career guide — is where the real risks on your first hitch are actually explained.</p>
    <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);margin-top:8px;">By <a href="/about/chris-cooper" style="color:var(--amber);">Chris Cooper</a>{safetyDateModified && <> · Updated {new Date(safetyDateModified).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' })}</>}</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="prose" style="max-width:900px;">
      <h2 id="what-actually-kills-and-injures" class="section-title" style="font-size:28px;">What Actually Causes Injury and Death</h2>
      <p>The single biggest killer of oil and gas workers in the US isn't anything on this list below — it's the drive to and from the location. Transportation incidents account for roughly 4 in 10 fatalities across the whole industry, more than any on-site hazard category on its own, according to the <a href="https://www.osha.gov/oil-and-gas-extraction/hazards" style="color:var(--amber);">OSHA oil and gas extraction hazard data</a> and the <a href="https://www.bls.gov/iif/fatal-injuries-tables.htm" style="color:var(--amber);">BLS Census of Fatal Occupational Injuries</a>. That's worth knowing before the rig-floor hazards below, since it's the one risk that has nothing to do with how careful you are once you're actually on location.</p>
      <p>Of the fatalities that do happen on site, OSHA's own figures put roughly 3 in every 5 in the struck-by, caught-in and caught-between bucket — a wider category than any single hazard below, covering moving vehicles on location, equipment swinging or falling, and high-pressure line failures together, not dropped objects alone. Globally, <a href="https://www.iogp.org/bookstore/portfolio-item/safety-performance-indicators/" style="color:var(--amber);">IOGP's own safety performance data</a> shows explosion, fire and burns as the leading cause of fatality industry-wide in recent reporting years, with dropped objects specifically the leading cause offshore — two different, more precise claims than "dropped objects cause the most injuries and deaths on rigs," which overstates what either data set actually shows.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col" style="margin-top:28px;">
      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:28px;">Major <span>Hazard Categories</span></h2>
        <div class="card-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;">
          <div class="card">
            <span class="card-icon">🚚</span>
            <h3>Transportation</h3>
            <p>The leading cause of fatality across the US oil and gas workforce overall — highway driving between locations, not a rig-floor hazard specifically. Fatigue management and vehicle condition matter as much as anything covered below.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="card-icon">💨</span>
            <h3>H₂S (Hydrogen Sulfide)</h3>
            <p>Colorless gas heavier than air. At 100–150 ppm, exposure is immediately dangerous to life and health; at 700+ ppm, a single breath can be fatal. High concentrations kill your sense of smell instantly.</p>
            <a href="/safety/h2s" class="card-link">Full H₂S Safety Guide →</a>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="card-icon">🔥</span>
            <h3>Fire & Explosion</h3>
            <p>Hydrocarbons from the wellbore, fuel tanks, and process equipment create constant fire risk. IOGP's global data names this the leading cause of fatality industry-wide in recent reporting years. Hot work requires a permit and gas testing immediately before starting.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="card-icon">⬇️</span>
            <h3>Struck-By, Caught-In and Dropped Objects</h3>
            <p>Together the largest category of on-site fatalities in US data. Dropped objects specifically are the leading cause of fatality offshore. Tools and equipment can fall from derrick height — all tools must be tethered on height work.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="card-icon">🔧</span>
            <h3>Stored Energy / LOTO</h3>
            <p>Hydraulic, pneumatic, electrical, and mechanical energy stored in rig systems. Lockout/Tagout (LOTO) procedures must be followed for all maintenance.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:28px;">PPE <span>Requirements</span></h2>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px;margin-bottom:28px;">
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--steel);border-radius:3px;"><span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--mist);">Hard Hat (EN 397 / ANSI Z89.1)</span><span class="status-badge status-safe">Always Required</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--steel);border-radius:3px;"><span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--mist);"><a href="/blog/oil-rig-boots" style="color:var(--amber);">Safety Boots</a> (S3 / ASTM F2413)</span><span class="status-badge status-safe">Always Required</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--steel);border-radius:3px;"><span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--mist);">FR Coveralls</span><span class="status-badge status-safe">Always Required</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--steel);border-radius:3px;"><span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--mist);">Safety Glasses / Face Shield</span><span class="status-badge status-safe">Always Required</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--steel);border-radius:3px;"><span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--mist);">H₂S Personal Monitor</span><span class="status-badge status-warn">Sour Wells</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--steel);border-radius:3px;"><span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--mist);">SCBA</span><span class="status-badge status-warn">Sour Wells / Fire</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--steel);border-radius:3px;"><span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--mist);">Fall Arrest Harness</span><span class="status-badge status-warn">Working at Height</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--steel);border-radius:3px;"><span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--mist);">Welding Helmet (Shade 10+)</span><span class="status-badge status-warn">Hot Work Only</span></div>
        </div>

        <p style="color:var(--fog);font-size:13px;line-height:1.7;margin-top:14px;">PPE selection on a rig isn't generic workplace policy — <a href="https://www.api.org/products-and-services/standards/important-standards-announcements/recommended-practice-54" style="color:var(--amber);">API RP 54</a> ties specific equipment to specific well-control and rig-floor hazards, on top of the baseline <a href="https://www.osha.gov/personal-protective-equipment/standards" style="color:var(--amber);">OSHA PPE standards</a> (1910.132 general requirements, plus eye/face, head and foot protection) that apply across any industrial site. A hazard assessment, not a checklist copied from another rig, is what's supposed to decide which items above are actually required for a given job.</p>

        <h3 style="font-family:var(--cond);font-weight:700;font-size:16px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);letter-spacing:0.04em;margin-bottom:12px;margin-top:22px;">Key Safety Systems</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--fog);font-size:13px;line-height:1.7;">Every offshore installation has a documented Emergency Response Plan including muster point assignment, lifeboat/liferaft stations, evacuation routes, fire and gas detection with automatic shutdown, and helicopter emergency procedures. Know yours before your first shift.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="max-width:900px;margin-top:28px;">
      <h2 id="permit-and-isolation" class="section-title" style="font-size:28px;">Permit to Work and Energy Isolation</h2>
      <p>Most of the hazards above don't hurt anyone on their own — they hurt someone when two jobs collide without anyone realizing it: welding starts while a line is still pressured up, or someone opens a valve a crew is working on down the other side. The permit-to-work system is the control that's supposed to prevent exactly that collision, by making one person formally confirm a job is safe to start before it does. See the <a href="/blog/permit-to-work-system" style="color:var(--amber);">full permit-to-work breakdown</a> for who signs what and where the system actually breaks down in practice.</p>
      <p>Lockout/Tagout for hydraulic, pneumatic, electrical and mechanical stored energy is usually the specific control a hot-work or maintenance permit requires before work starts, not a separate system running in parallel. The UK's Health and Safety Executive has published research finding that permit-system failures — not the underlying hazard itself — were the single largest cause of maintenance-related incidents in the industries it studied, which is the real argument for treating the paperwork as seriously as the physical isolation it authorizes.</p>

      <h2 id="reporting-and-culture" class="section-title" style="font-size:28px;margin-top:8px;">Reporting Near-Misses, Not Just Injuries</h2>
      <p>The hazards above only get fixed on a given rig if someone actually reports the near-miss before it becomes an injury — a loose connection, a tool left untethered, a permit signed off without actually checking the line was bled down. Most drilling contractors run some version of a safety-observation-card program for exactly this, built on the same underlying idea whether the specific form is called a STOP card or something else: a worker who sees a hazard writes it up, on the spot, without waiting for an incident to force the conversation. See the <a href="/blog/stop-card-system" style="color:var(--amber);">full STOP card breakdown</a> for where the methodology came from and what actually makes a card worth writing versus a box ticked for its own sake.</p>
      <p>None of the systems on this page — PPE, permits, LOTO, observation cards — work in isolation from each other. A permit that's signed correctly but ignores a near-miss report from the same job, or PPE that's worn correctly but issued without the hazard assessment that was supposed to justify it, is a system with a real gap in it even though every individual piece looks compliant on paper.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Explore</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Safety <span>Deep Dives</span></h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">💨</span>
        <h3>H₂S Safety</h3>
        <p>Detection, exposure limits, escape procedures, and personal monitoring requirements for sour gas.</p>
        <a href="/safety/h2s" class="card-link">Read More →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🦺</span>
        <h3>Rig Floor PPE</h3>
        <p>What the OSHA assessment requirement, ANSI performance ratings and API drilling guidance actually demand — and how they fit together on the drill floor.</p>
        <a href="/blog/rig-floor-ppe-guide" class="card-link">Read More →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">📋</span>
        <h3>Permit to Work</h3>
        <p>How the permit system controls hot work, confined space entry and energy isolation — who signs, what it covers, and where it breaks down.</p>
        <a href="/blog/permit-to-work-system" class="card-link">Read More →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🛑</span>
        <h3>STOP Cards</h3>
        <p>Safety observation cards: where the methodology came from, how drilling contractors adapted it, and what makes a card worth writing.</p>
        <a href="/blog/stop-card-system" class="card-link">Read More →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SourcesStandards lang="en" ids={['oshaOilGasHazards', 'blsCfoi', 'iogpSpi', 'apiRp54', 'oshaPpe', 'hseHsg250']} />
</BaseLayout>
